Now rewrite epochPair to:
- return undefined when only one of the pair is present (unknown, not an error);
- accept an optional UTC offset in the time;
- keep 'bad' for unknown markers or malformed values.

New implementation:

```ts
/**
 * What: turns a PGN date and clock into a UTC epoch, honouring an explicit zone offset.
 * Why: chess.com writes `EndTime` as `18:51:25 GMT+0000` and often omits `EndDate`, and players in
 *      other zones write their own offset; the clock alone is still not a date, so it stays unknown.
 * Removed: a valid export from any timezone would be rejected outright.
 */
function epochPair(date: string | undefined, time: string | undefined): number | undefined | 'bad' {
  if (date === undefined && time === undefined) return undefined;
  // A half pair cannot make a UTC instant. It is unknown, not invalid: nothing may guess the date.
  if (date === undefined || time === undefined) return undefined;
  if (date === UNKNOWN_DATE && time === UNKNOWN_TIME) return undefined;
  if (date.includes('?') || time.includes('?')) return 'bad';
  const day = /^(\d{4})\.(\d{2})\.(\d{2})$/.exec(date.trim());
  const clock = /^(\d{2}):(\d{2}):(\d{2})(?:\s*(?:(GMT|UTC)\s*)?([+-])(\d{2}):?(\d{2})|Z)?$/i.exec(time.trim());
```

Hmm the regex with alternation group is messy. Simpler: split time into `base` and `zone` at the first space:
```ts
const [base, ...rest] = time.trim().split(/\s+/);
const zone = rest.join(' ');
const clock = /^(\d{2}):(\d{2}):(\d{2})$/.exec(base);
```
zone: undefined | '' | 'Z' | 'GMT+0000' | 'UTC-05:00' | '+0530' | '-05:00'.

Parse zone:
```ts
function zoneMinutes(zone: string | undefined): number | 'bad' {
  if (zone === undefined || zone.length === 0) return 0;   // PGN's UTCDate/UTCTime are UTC; a bare clock is taken as UTC
  if (/^z$/i.test(zone)) return 0;
  const match = /^(?:GMT|UTC)?\s*([+-])(\d{2}):?(\d{2})$/i.exec(zone);
  if (!match) return 'bad';
  const hours = Number(match[2]); const minutes = Number(match[3]);
  if (hours > 14 || minutes > 59) return 'bad';
  const total = hours * 60 + minutes;
  return match[1] === '-' ? -total : total;
}
```
Careful: if `base` isn't a clock because the time is like "18:51:25GMT+0000" (no space) → split fails. Handle by regex on the whole string instead:

```ts
const clock = /^(\d{2}):(\d{2}):(\d{2})\s*(.*)$/.exec(time.trim());
if (!clock) return 'bad';
const zone = clock[4]?.trim();
```
Then zoneMinutes(zone || undefined).

